    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Motivation and Scope\n\nServing large models at low latency and high throughput requires partitioning the model across multiple accelerators. Two dominant sharding strategies are:\n\n- **Tensor parallelism (TP)**: splits individual weight matrices (and activations) across devices. Every device holds a slice of *every* layer; all devices participate in every forward pass.\n- **Expert parallelism (EP)**: used in Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models. Each device holds a *disjoint subset of experts*; tokens are routed to whichever device owns the relevant expert. Devices not involved in a given token's expert computation stay idle for that token (unless combined with other parallelism).\n\nIn practice, production inference stacks (vLLM, TensorRT-LLM, DeepSpeed-MI, Megatron-based servers) combine **TP + EP + data parallelism (DP)**, and often pipeline parallelism (PP). This note focuses on the mechanics of TP and EP, their data flow, the collectives they rely on, and how to verify correctness.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 Sharding scheme\n\nConsider a transformer block. The two main matmuls are:\n\n- Attention projection: `Y = X \u00b7 W` where `W \u2208 \u211d^{d\u00d73d}` (QKV) and `W \u2208 \u211d^{d\u00d7d}` (output proj)\n- MLP: `Y = X \u00b7 W\u2081` (d \u2192 4d), activation, `Y = Y \u00b7 W\u2082` (4d \u2192 d)\n\nMegatron-style TP shards these as:\n\n| Weight | Shard type | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| QKV projection (`W_qkv`) | **Column-parallel** (split output dim) | Each rank computes attention for a subset of heads \u2014 no communication needed before attention |\n| Attention",
      "reasoning": "",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
